.TH SYS_PROC_FDS 2 "May 2026" "Substrate" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
sys_proc_fds \- list file descriptors for a process
.SH LIBRARY
System library (libsys, \-lsys)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/sysinfo.h>
.PP
.BI "int sys_proc_fds(pid_t " pid ", sys_fd_t *" fds ", size_t *" count ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sys_proc_fds ()
enumerates the open file descriptors of process
.IR pid .
For each populated entry of the process's
.B fds[]
table the kernel writes a
.B sys_fd_t
record:
.PP
.RS
.nf
typedef struct sys_fd {
    int      fd;            /* descriptor index */
    char     path[256];     /* best-effort path */
    uint32_t flags;         /* f_flag bits */
} sys_fd_t;
.fi
.RE
.PP
On entry
.I *count
is the array capacity; on return it is the true number of open
descriptors.  Caller may probe with
.I fds
=
.B NULL
and
.I *count
= 0.
.PP
.I pid
= 0 means the calling process.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 on success, a negated errno on failure.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ESRCH
.I pid
does not refer to an existing process.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I fds
or
.I count
points outside the caller's address space.
.SH NOTES
.B path
is filled from the underlying file's
.B f_path
field which is best-effort: pipes, sockets, and anonymous file
descriptors return an empty string.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_proc_info (2),
.BR sys_proc_maps (2),
.BR open (2)
